# Selecto JSON Operations Workbook

## Setup 1) Install Dependencies

Run this cell first. It installs the local `selecto_livebooks` project and its
current Selecto dependencies for this Livebook session.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install!()
```

## Overview

This workbook focuses on JSON/JSONB querying patterns in Selecto.

1. Project JSON fields into relational columns (`json_select`)
2. Filter rows by JSON content (`json_filter`)
3. Order rows by JSON paths (`json_order_by`)
4. Mix JSON operations with standard relational filters and sorting

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  row[Product row]
  metadata[metadata jsonb]
  extract[Extract JSON paths]
  filter[JSON filter]
  order[JSON-aware ordering]
  out[Combined result]

  row --> metadata --> extract --> out
  metadata --> filter --> out
  metadata --> order --> out
```

```mermaid
graph TD
  m[metadata]
  pb[price_band]
  wh[warehouse]
  zone[zone]
  flags[flags]
  flag0[flags[0]]

  m --> pb
  m --> wh --> zone
  m --> flags --> flag0
```

Before running, initialize the sample database:

```bash
mix setup
```

## Setup 2) Connect to Repo and Build Product Domain

This cell defines a local Repo and a product domain that includes `metadata`
and `tags`. PostgreSQL stores metadata as `jsonb`; the Selecto domain uses the
semantic type `:json` so structured dot paths and selection shapes can resolve
its nested fields. Storage type and domain type need not have the same name.

```elixir
defmodule JsonLab.Repo do
  use Ecto.Repo,
    otp_app: :json_lab_livebook,
    adapter: Ecto.Adapters.Postgres
end

defmodule JsonLab.ProductDomain do
  @moduledoc false

  def domain do
    %{
      name: "Products",
      source: %{
        source_table: "products",
        primary_key: :id,
        fields: [
          :id,
          :name,
          :sku,
          :description,
          :price,
          :stock_quantity,
          :reorder_level,
          :active,
          :featured,
          :tags,
          :metadata,
          :category_id,
          :supplier_id,
          :inserted_at
        ],
        columns: %{
          id: %{type: :integer},
          name: %{type: :string},
          sku: %{type: :string},
          description: %{type: :text},
          price: %{type: :decimal},
          stock_quantity: %{type: :integer},
          reorder_level: %{type: :integer},
          active: %{type: :boolean},
          featured: %{type: :boolean},
          tags: %{type: {:array, :string}},
          metadata: %{
            type: :json,
            schema: %{
              "price_band" => %{type: :string},
              "warehouse" => %{type: :object, schema: %{"zone" => %{type: :string}}}
            }
          },
          category_id: %{type: :integer},
          supplier_id: %{type: :integer},
          inserted_at: %{type: :utc_datetime}
        },
        associations: %{}
      },
      schemas: %{},
      joins: %{},
      default_selected: ["name", "sku", "price", "metadata"],
      default_order_by: [{"name", :asc}]
    }
  end
end

repo_config = [
  database: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB", "selecto_livebooks_dev"),
  username: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_USER", "postgres"),
  password: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PASS", "postgres"),
  hostname: System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_HOST", "localhost"),
  port: String.to_integer(System.get_env("SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB_PORT", "5432")),
  pool_size: 5
]

case Process.whereis(JsonLab.Repo) do
  nil -> {:ok, _pid} = JsonLab.Repo.start_link(repo_config)
  _pid -> :ok
end

config = %{
  repo: JsonLab.Repo,
  product_domain: JsonLab.ProductDomain.domain()
}

{:ok, result} = Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, "select count(*) from products", [])
[[product_count]] = result.rows
IO.puts("Connected. Products in dataset: #{product_count}")

config
```

## Setup 3) Seed Metadata for Demonstrations

This cell refreshes `products.metadata` with predictable JSON keys used in the examples.

```elixir
seed_result =
  config.repo.query!("""
  UPDATE products
  SET metadata = jsonb_build_object(
    'price_band',
    CASE
      WHEN price >= 100 THEN 'premium'
      WHEN price >= 50 THEN 'mid'
      ELSE 'budget'
    END,
    'warehouse',
    jsonb_build_object(
      'zone',
      CASE WHEN stock_quantity <= reorder_level THEN 'restock' ELSE 'in_stock' END,
      'bin', concat('A-', lpad((id % 25)::text, 2, '0'))
    ),
    'flags',
    jsonb_build_array(
      CASE WHEN active THEN 'active' ELSE 'inactive' END,
      CASE WHEN featured THEN 'featured' ELSE 'standard' END
    )
  )
  """)

IO.puts("Metadata rows refreshed: #{seed_result.num_rows}")
```

## Setup 4) Define JSON Helpers

This helper cell prints SQL/params and executes a query with a compact preview.

```elixir
defmodule JsonLab.Helpers do
  @moduledoc false

  def explain(label, query), do: Selecto.Livebook.explain(label, query)

  def run(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    Selecto.Livebook.run(label, query, preview_count: preview_count)
  end

  def run_shape(label, query, preview_count \\ 10) do
    explain(label, query)
    IO.inspect(query.set.selection_shape, label: "Compiled shape", pretty: true)

    case Selecto.execute_shape(query) do
      {:ok, shaped_rows} = ok ->
        IO.puts("Shaped rows: #{length(shaped_rows)}")
        IO.inspect(Enum.take(shaped_rows, preview_count), label: "Shape preview (up to #{preview_count} rows)")
        ok

      {:error, error} = failure ->
        IO.puts("Error: #{inspect(error)}")
        failure
    end
  end
end
```

## Setup 5) Current Expression DSL Imports

This workbook keeps the JSON-specific operators explicit, but now uses the
current expression DSL for the surrounding relational select, filter, and order
clauses.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

:ok
```

## 1) Extract JSON Fields into Select Columns

Use `json_select` to project JSON paths into result columns alongside standard relational fields.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

json_extract_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", as: "price_band"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.flags[0]", as: "primary_flag"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON extraction", json_extract_query)
```

## 2) Filter by JSON Containment

Use `json_filter` with containment to keep only products whose JSON metadata declares `price_band = premium`.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

json_filter_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", as: "price_band"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.json_filter({:json_contains, "metadata", %{"price_band" => "premium"}})
  |> Selecto.filter(where(active == true))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON containment filter", json_filter_query)
```

## 3) Order by a JSON Path

Apply `json_order_by` to sort by JSON path output after relational ordering.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

json_order_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.bin", as: "warehouse_bin"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.filter(where(active == true))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([asc(name)]))
  |> Selecto.json_order_by({:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON path ordering", json_order_query)
```

## 4) Combined Relational + JSON Query

Combine relational filters, JSON filters, JSON projections, and mixed ordering in one query.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

combined_json_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price, active]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", as: "price_band"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.flags[1]", as: "secondary_flag"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.filter(where(price >= 25))
  |> Selecto.json_filter({:json_contains, "metadata", %{"warehouse" => %{"zone" => "in_stock"}}})
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price)]))
  |> Selecto.json_order_by({:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", :asc})
  |> Selecto.limit(15)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("Combined JSON + relational query", combined_json_query)
```

## 5) JSON Path Existence and Path-Match Filters

Use JSON existence checks for optional keys, then add path-based value matching.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

optional_key_seed =
  config.repo.query!("""
  UPDATE products
  SET metadata =
    CASE
      WHEN featured THEN jsonb_set(
        coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb),
        '{promo_code}',
        to_jsonb(concat('PROMO-', id::text)),
        true
      )
      ELSE coalesce(metadata, '{}'::jsonb) - 'promo_code'
    END
  """)

IO.puts("Optional promo_code keys refreshed: #{optional_key_seed.num_rows}")

json_existence_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price, featured]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.promo_code", as: "promo_code"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.json_filter([
    {:json_path_exists, "metadata", "$.promo_code"},
    {:json_contains, "metadata", %{"warehouse" => %{"zone" => "in_stock"}}}
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON path existence + path match", json_existence_query)
```

## 6) JSON Filter vs Relational Filter (Same Rule)

`price_band = premium` was seeded from `price >= 100`. This section compares JSON and relational implementations of the same business rule.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

premium_by_json_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([{:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", as: "price_band"}])
  |> Selecto.json_filter({:json_contains, "metadata", %{"price_band" => "premium"}})
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price), asc(id)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

premium_by_relational_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price]))
  |> Selecto.filter(where(price >= 100))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price), asc(id)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(20)

{:ok, {json_rows, _, _}} = JsonLab.Helpers.run("Premium by JSON price_band", premium_by_json_query, 5)
{:ok, {rel_rows, _, _}} = JsonLab.Helpers.run("Premium by relational price threshold", premium_by_relational_query, 5)

IO.puts("JSON rows: #{length(json_rows)}")
IO.puts("Relational rows: #{length(rel_rows)}")
# JSON adds one extra projected column; compare the common fields and order.
true = Enum.map(json_rows, &Enum.take(&1, 3)) == rel_rows
IO.puts("The ordered product rows match, not just their count.")
```

## 7) Shaped Output with `json_select/2` + `select_shape/2`

Project JSON-derived columns and package them into nested output tuples for structured rendering.
When shaping, use explicit field selectors for JSON paths (rather than alias-only names) so field resolution remains valid.
`select_shape/2` is the final projection step, so a prior `select/2` is unnecessary in the same pipeline.

```elixir
import Selecto.ExprMacros

json_shaped_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.price_band", as: "price_band"},
    {:json_extract_text, "metadata", "$.warehouse.zone", as: "warehouse_zone"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.select_shape([
    "name",
    {"sku", "price"},
    {
      {:field, "metadata.price_band", "price_band"},
      {:field, "metadata.warehouse.zone", "warehouse_zone"}
    }
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price), asc(id)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run_shape("Shaped JSON projection", json_shaped_query, 10)
```

## 9) JSON Aggregates by Price Band

Aggregate JSON payloads for grouped reporting while using metadata-derived
price bands as dimensions.

```elixir
json_category_agg_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select([
    {:field, "metadata.price_band", "price_band"},
    {:field, {:count, "*"}, "product_count"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.json_select([
    {:json_agg, "name", as: "product_names_json"},
    {:json_object_agg, "sku", "price", as: "sku_price_json"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.group_by(["metadata.price_band"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({{:count, "*"}, :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON aggregates by price band", json_category_agg_query, 10)
```

## 10) Ranked Metadata Cohorts

Extract metadata, then add a window rank within the extracted cohort.

```elixir
json_window_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select([
    "name",
    "sku",
    "price",
    {:field, {:raw_sql, "metadata ->> 'price_band'"}, "price_band"},
    {:field,
     {:raw_sql,
      "ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY metadata ->> 'price_band' ORDER BY selecto_root.price DESC)"},
     "price_band_rank"}
  ])
  |> Selecto.order_by({{:raw_sql, "metadata ->> 'price_band'"}, :asc})
  |> Selecto.order_by({"price", :desc})
  |> Selecto.limit(30)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("JSON metadata cohort ranking", json_window_query, 20)
```

## 11) Side-by-Side JSON and Relational Results

Compare a JSON-extracted projection with ordinary relational fields in a compact
Kino table.

```elixir
relational_compare_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku, price, active]))
  |> Selecto.order_by(order_by([desc(price), asc(id)]))
  |> Selecto.limit(10)

{:ok, json_rows} = JsonLab.Helpers.run_shape("JSON side", json_shaped_query, 10)
{:ok, {rel_rows, rel_columns, _aliases}} = Selecto.execute(relational_compare_query)
true = length(json_rows) == length(rel_rows)

comparison_rows =
  Enum.zip(json_rows, rel_rows)
  |> Enum.map(fn {json_row, rel_row} ->
    relational_map = Enum.zip(rel_columns, rel_row) |> Map.new()

    %{
      product: relational_map["name"],
      relational_active: relational_map["active"],
      relational_price: relational_map["price"],
      json_shape: inspect(json_row)
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(comparison_rows)
```

## 12) Validated JSON Path Inputs

Treat JSON paths as user input only after checking them against an allow-list.

```elixir
allowed_json_paths = %{
  "price_band" => "$.price_band",
  "warehouse_zone" => "$.warehouse.zone",
  "secondary_flag" => "$.flags[1]"
}

requested_path_key = "warehouse_zone"

case Map.fetch(allowed_json_paths, requested_path_key) do
  {:ok, json_path} ->
    validated_path_query =
      Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
      |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku]))
      |> Selecto.json_select([{:json_extract_text, "metadata", json_path, as: requested_path_key}])
      |> Selecto.limit(8)

    JsonLab.Helpers.run("Validated JSON path input", validated_path_query, 8)

  :error ->
    IO.puts("Rejected unsupported JSON path key: #{requested_path_key}")
end
```

## 13) Text Extraction vs Typed Numeric Casts

JSON text extraction returns strings. Cast when downstream sorting or arithmetic
needs numeric behavior.

```elixir
{:ok, cast_comparison} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    name,
    metadata ->> 'stock_snapshot' AS stock_snapshot_text,
    (metadata ->> 'stock_snapshot')::integer AS stock_snapshot_int
  FROM (
    SELECT
      name,
      jsonb_set(metadata, '{stock_snapshot}', to_jsonb(stock_quantity), true) AS metadata
    FROM products
  ) enriched_products
  ORDER BY stock_snapshot_int DESC
  LIMIT 10
  """, [])

cast_comparison.rows
```

## 14) API-Ready Maps from JSON Aggregates

Convert aggregate JSON rows into plain maps that are ready to return from an API
or encode as JSON. `{:field, selector, alias}` supplies Selecto result metadata;
it does not necessarily rename PostgreSQL's returned column. The `:maps`
transformer applies that metadata, unlike zipping raw database column names.

```elixir
{:ok, aggregate_maps} = Selecto.execute(json_category_agg_query, format: :maps)

api_ready_json_aggregates =
  Enum.map(aggregate_maps, fn row_map ->
    %{
      price_band: Map.fetch!(row_map, "price_band"),
      product_count: Map.fetch!(row_map, "product_count"),
      product_names: Map.fetch!(row_map, "product_names_json"),
      sku_prices: Map.fetch!(row_map, "sku_price_json")
    }
  end)

Jason.encode!(api_ready_json_aggregates)
```

## 15) JSON Path Select Input

Connect the path allow-list to a `Kino.Input.select`, with a verification-safe
fallback for non-interactive execution.

```elixir
path_options = Enum.map(Map.keys(allowed_json_paths), &{&1, &1})
path_input = Kino.Input.select("JSON path", path_options, default: "price_band")

selected_path_key =
  try do
    Kino.Input.read(path_input) || "price_band"
  rescue
    _ -> "price_band"
  end

selected_path_query =
  Selecto.configure(config.product_domain, config.repo)
  |> Selecto.select(select([name, sku]))
  |> Selecto.json_select([{:json_extract_text, "metadata", allowed_json_paths[selected_path_key], as: selected_path_key}])
  |> Selecto.limit(6)

JsonLab.Helpers.run("Selected JSON path", selected_path_query, 6)
```

## 16) Typed JSON Casts for Dates and Booleans

Cast JSON text into booleans and timestamps when downstream code needs typed
comparison or sorting.

```elixir
{:ok, typed_cast_rows} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT
    name,
    (metadata -> 'flags' ? 'featured') AS featured_flag,
    inserted_at,
    to_jsonb(inserted_at)::text AS inserted_at_json_text
  FROM products
  ORDER BY inserted_at DESC
  LIMIT 8
  """, [])

typed_cast_rows.rows
```

## 17) Merge Relational and JSON Aggregate Maps

Combine relational totals with JSON aggregate payloads into API-style maps.

```elixir
{:ok, relational_status_totals} =
  Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(config.repo, """
  SELECT status, count(*) AS order_count, sum(total) AS gross_total
  FROM orders
  GROUP BY status
  ORDER BY gross_total DESC
  """, [])

merged_api_maps = %{
  order_statuses:
    Enum.map(relational_status_totals.rows, fn [status, order_count, gross_total] ->
      %{status: status, order_count: order_count, gross_total: gross_total}
    end),
  product_price_bands: api_ready_json_aggregates
}

Jason.encode!(merged_api_maps)
```

